1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,220
TUTOR: OK, Jim. You wanted to see me about your textile design project.

2
00:00:07,220 --> 00:00:15,230
JIM: That's right. I've been looking at how a range of natural dyes can be used to colour fabrics like cotton and wool.

3
00:00:15,230 --> 00:00:17,960
TUTOR: Why did you choose that topic?

4
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:21,620
JIM: Well, I got a lot of useful ideas from the museum

5
00:00:21,900 --> 00:00:24,092
you know, at that exhibition of textiles.

6
00:00:24,092 --> 00:00:27,642
But I've always been interested in anything to do with colour.

7
00:00:27,642 --> 00:00:31,100
Years ago, I went to a carpet shop with my parents

8
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:33,451
when we were on holiday in Turkey,

9
00:00:33,451 --> 00:00:35,710
and I remember all the amazing colours.

10
00:00:35,710 --> 00:00:39,720
TUTOR: They might not all have been natural dyes.

11
00:00:39,720 --> 00:00:44,441
JIM: Maybe not, but for the project I decided to follow it up.

12
00:00:44,441 --> 00:00:51,340
And I found a great book about a botanic garden in California that specialises in plants used for dyes.

13
00:00:51,340 --> 00:00:57,690
TUTOR: OK. So, in your project, you had to include a practical investigation.

14
00:00:57,690 --> 00:01:01,450
JIM: Yeah. At first I couldn't decide on my variables.

15
00:01:01,450 --> 00:01:07,090
I was going to just look at one type of fibre for example, like cotton...

16
00:01:07,090 --> 00:01:10,940
TUTOR:... and see how different types of dyes affected it?

17
00:01:10,940 --> 00:01:14,780
JIM: Yes. Then I decided to include others as well

18
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:18,550
so I looked at cotton and wool and nylon.

19
00:01:18,550 --> 00:01:21,250
TUTOR: With just one type of dye?

20
00:01:21,250 --> 00:01:26,090
JIM: Various types, including some that weren't natural, for comparison.

21
00:01:26,090 --> 00:01:27,700
TUTOR: OK.

22
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:30,664
JIM: So, I did the experiments last week.

23
00:01:30,664 --> 00:01:32,887
I used some ready-made natural dyes,

24
00:01:32,887 --> 00:01:36,520
I found a website which supplied them

25
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:41,040
they came in just a few days, but I also made some of my own.

26
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:43,820
TUTOR: That must have taken quite a bit of time.

27
00:01:43,820 --> 00:01:48,884
JIM: Yes, I'd thought it'd just be a matter of a teaspoon or so of dye,

28
00:01:48,884 --> 00:01:51,416
and actually that wasn't the case at all.

29
00:01:51,416 --> 00:01:54,780
Like I was using one vegetable, beetroot

30
00:01:54,880 --> 00:01:58,378
for a red dye, and I had to chop up a whole pile of it.

31
00:01:58,378 --> 00:02:00,910
So it all took longer than I'd expected.

32
00:02:00,910 --> 00:02:04,630
TUTOR: One possibility is to use food colourings.

33
00:02:04,630 --> 00:02:09,030
JIM: I did use one. That was a yellow dye, an artificial one.

34
00:02:09,030 --> 00:02:10,960
TUTOR: Tartrazine?

35
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:13,920
JIM: Yeah. I used it on cotton first

36
00:02:14,340 --> 00:02:16,965
It came out a great colour, but when I rinsed the material,

37
00:02:16,965 --> 00:02:18,640
the colour just washed away

38
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,670
I'd been going to try it out on nylon, but I abandoned that idea.

39
00:02:22,670 --> 00:02:25,980
TUTOR: Were you worried about health issues?

40
00:02:25,980 --> 00:02:29,900
JIM: I'd thought if it's a legal food colouring, it must be safe.

41
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:34,440
TUTOR: Well, it can occasionally cause allergic reactions, I believe.

42
00:02:34,440 --> 00:02:37,710
TUTOR: So what natural dyes did you look at?

43
00:02:37,710 --> 00:02:39,940
JIM: Well, one was turmeric.

44
00:02:39,940 --> 00:02:43,800
The colour's great, it's a really strong yellow

45
00:02:44,380 --> 00:02:46,630
It's generally used in dishes like curry.

46
00:02:46,630 --> 00:02:50,013
TUTOR: It's meant to be quite good for your health when eaten,

47
00:02:50,013 --> 00:02:53,600
but you might find it's not permanent when it's used as a dye

48
00:02:53,880 --> 00:02:55,370
a few washes, and it's gone.

49
00:02:55,370 --> 00:03:00,140
JIM: Right. I used beetroot as a dye for wool

50
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:02,891
When I chop up beetroot to eat I always end up

51
00:03:02,891 --> 00:03:08,980
with bright red hands, but the wool ended up just a sort of watery cream shade. Disappointing.

52
00:03:08,980 --> 00:03:13,240
TUTOR: There's a natural dye called Tyrian purple. Have you heard of that?

53
00:03:13,240 --> 00:03:15,768
JIM: Yes. It comes from a shellfish,

54
00:03:15,768 --> 00:03:18,820
and it was worn in ancient times

55
00:03:18,820 --> 00:03:23,350
but only by important people as it was so rare. I didn't use it.

56
00:03:23,350 --> 00:03:26,220
TUTOR: It fell out of use centuries ago

57
00:03:26,220 --> 00:03:29,250
though one researcher managed to get hold of some recently.

58
00:03:29,250 --> 00:03:33,183
But that shade of purple can be produced by chemical dyes nowadays.

59
00:03:33,183 --> 00:03:35,150
Did you use any black dyes?

60
00:03:35,150 --> 00:03:38,720
JIM: Logwood. That was quite complicated

61
00:03:39,140 --> 00:03:41,860
I had to prepare the fabric so the dye would take.

62
00:03:41,860 --> 00:03:44,640
TUTOR: I hope you were careful to wear gloves.

63
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:47,530
JIM: Yes. I know the danger with that dye.

64
00:03:47,530 --> 00:03:51,255
TUTOR: Good. It can be extremely dangerous if it's ingested.

65
00:03:51,255 --> 00:03:56,470
Now, presumably you had a look at an insect-based dye? Like cochineal, for example?

66
00:03:56,470 --> 00:03:59,800
JIM: Yes. I didn't actually make that

67
00:04:00,340 --> 00:04:03,458
I didn't have time to start crushing up insects to get the red colour

68
00:04:03,458 --> 00:04:06,080
and anyway they're not available here

69
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:09,448
but I managed to get the dye quite easily from a website.

70
00:04:09,448 --> 00:04:11,180
But it cost a fortune

71
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:15,770
I can see why it's generally just used in cooking, and in small quantities.

72
00:04:15,770 --> 00:04:21,100
TUTOR: Yes, it's very effective, but that's precisely why it's not used as a dye.

73
00:04:21,100 --> 00:04:24,103
JIM: I also read about using metal oxide.

74
00:04:24,103 --> 00:04:27,520
Apparently you can allow iron to rust

75
00:04:27,520 --> 00:04:30,860
while it's in contact with the fabric, and that colours it.

76
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:33,229
TUTOR: Yes, that works well for dying cotton.

77
00:04:33,229 --> 00:04:37,374
But you have to be careful as the metal can actually affect the fabric

78
00:04:37,374 --> 00:04:42,703
and so you can't expect to get a lot of wear out of fabrics treated in this way.

79
00:04:42,703 --> 00:04:45,663
And the colours are quite subtle, not everyone likes them.

80
00:04:45,663 --> 00:04:48,920
Anyway, it looks as if you've done a lot of work...
